course: dictionary
lesson: 12048
title: Circumvent: going around the problem
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To circumvent something is to find a clever way around it instead of facing it head on. «The road was blocked by a fallen tree, so the bus circumvented it by taking the country lane.» The bus did not move the tree. It went around the problem.
explain: Sometimes circumventing a rule is not quite fair. «Mia circumvented the game's age limit by typing a false birthday.» She got round the rule, but she broke its spirit. Ask yourself: did the person avoid the problem cleverly, or sneakily?
explain: Circumvent is a formal word, so you will meet it more in news and grown-up writing than in chatting. Everyday English says "get round" or "get around". If you read «circumvented the rule» and think "got around the rule", you have understood it. Use the swap check: put your everyday words in and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Circumvent means to find a way around something that is in the way.
fact: Circumventing a rule often means avoiding it in a way that is not quite fair.
fact: «Circumvent» is a formal word; everyday English says "get round" or "get around".
fact: In the news, people may talk about «circumventing the law» or «circumventing parental controls».
fact: Circumvent is a verb: circumvents, circumvented, circumventing.
exercise: «The workers circumvented the flooded road by taking the hill path.» What does circumvented mean here? | Stopped and waited for the water to go | Swam across the river | *Went around the problem
exercise: Which everyday words mean about the same as circumvent? | Shout loudly | Fall asleep | *Get round | Go hungry
exercise: «Sam circumvented the rule by sneaking in through the back door.» Why might the author choose circumvented? | *It shows Sam avoided the rule in a sneaky way | It shows Sam was very hungry | It shows Sam asked for permission
test: «The bridge was closed, so the driver circumvented the town centre.» What does circumvented mean? | *Went around the town centre | Bought a gift in the town centre | Sat down in the town centre
test: Which sentence shows circumventing in a not-quite-fair way? | Ali helped carry the chairs to the hall | *Ali got round the school's password rule by guessing a teacher's login | Ali read his book in the library
exam: Which is the best meaning of circumvent in «She circumvented the problem by finding a shorter route»? | *To avoid the problem by finding a clever way around it | To sing loudly about it | To make the problem bigger | To run away from home
